The bell rang, and I hurried down the corridor, my boots clicking on the cold tiles. I was just another teenager in a hoodie, but inside, my ears still twitched when the wind blew through the school gates. My name was Elias, and I was a wood elf who had learned to hide my magic behind a facade of ordinary school life.

In class, I sat near the back, pretending to doodle in my notebook while my fingers itched to weave tiny vines around the desk. My friends thought I was quiet, but I was just listening to the rhythm of the trees in the park outside. Sometimes, during lunch, I would sneak outside and let my leaves shimmer in the sunlight, feeling the earth hum beneath my feet.

The hardest part was the secret. I worried that if anyone found out, they would see me as different, not just a student. But I kept my magic small—a leaf that grew on my pencil, a breeze that rustled the leaves—and I kept my heart open to the world. School was loud and chaotic, but for me, it was also a place where I could be both human and something more.

One afternoon, a friend asked why I always looked at the trees. I smiled and said, "They remind me that things grow quietly, even when no one is watching." She didn't understand, but she listened. That was enough.

I didn't know what would happen next, but I knew I would keep my secret, and I would keep growing—slowly, like a tree in the wind.

